# Implementation Plan

Created March 1, 2026. This captures the phased plan for building the footballFrontEnd application. Priorities may shift — update this document as direction changes.

## Current State

### footballBackEnd (fully deployed and functional)
- AWS Lambda (Node.js 22.x) + API Gateway + RDS PostgreSQL
- 4 GET endpoints: `/players`, `/players/{id}`, `/players/{id}/stats`, `/players/{id}/roster-history`
- Cognito User Pool with Hosted UI, PKCE app client (no secret), authorization code flow
- API Gateway JWT authorizer validates **Cognito ID tokens** (not access tokens) against the app client ID
- Data loaded: 2023-2025 seasons, ~2,500 players, ~20,600 stat rows
- Daily sync Lambda via EventBridge + manual load/clear scripts
- 63 automated tests (37 unit + 26 integration) passing

### footballFrontEnd (Phase 1 complete as of March 1, 2026)
- Next.js 16 with App Router, Tailwind v4, Vitest, Storybook
- Design system foundation: Button, Badge primitives with tests/stories
- Design tokens (brew/vegas palette, typography)
- TypeScript types for Player, League, Matchup
- **Auth complete**: Cognito PKCE flow via BFF, iron-session encrypted cookies, middleware route protection, useSession hook
- **BFF proxy complete**: 4 data routes proxying to AWS API Gateway with ID token auth + automatic token refresh
- **API client complete**: Typed fetch wrapper for all BFF endpoints
- **32 design system tests passing**, TypeScript and Next.js build clean

## Known Issue: ID Token vs. Access Token

The API Gateway JWT authorizer validates **ID tokens**, not access tokens. Cognito access tokens set the audience to the User Pool URL, but the authorizer checks against the **client ID**, which only matches ID tokens.

The BFF must send the **ID token** (not access token) as `Authorization: Bearer` to the AWS API. The `bff.mdc` and `tech-stack.mdc` docs have been corrected to reflect this (previously referenced `accessToken`).

